> Fixes https://github.com/rust-lang/rust/issues/137740.

I think you might have misunderstood the issue description. "The term of what is meant by anchors is borrowed from" a description of this style of anchor links. It doesn't say anything about footnotes.

```markdown
/// Consider [panics](std::alloc::GlobalAllocator#Panics) on allocation.
//                                               ^^^^^^^ anchor
```

I'm sorry, but the information you need is just not in the event stream. You have to parse the source. Here's another counterexample:

```text
//! [^a ***b] foobar [^c*** d]
//~^ ERROR: no footnote definition matching this footnote
//~| ERROR: no footnote definition matching this footnote
//! [^e ***f] foobar [^g*** h]
//!
//! [^e ***f]: test footnote
//! [^g*** h]: test footnote
```

And I've pushed a parser that works correctly[^1] to my own branch here: https://github.com/notriddle/rust/commit/7a3654192972874a1e77d96f6067e2f6a7ad89e9

[^1]:

    <details><summary>There's still a couple of bugs in my version of this lint. Can you figure out where it is without me telling you?</summary>

    Both parsers, yours and mine, only produce a warning if the opening `[` in the broken footnote appears as text. The following code is a counterexample, because if pulldown-cmark parses `[^foot]` as a footnote, which it does, then `[^broken]` must be a broken footnote, yet there's no warning.

    ```text
    [^foot][link] [^broken][link]

    [link]: https://example.com
    [^foot]: note
    ```

    However, this case is so convoluted and obscure that, while [commonmark-hs](https://pandoc.org/try/?params=%7B%22text%22%3A%22%5B%5Efoot%5D%5Blink%5D+%5B%5Ebroken%5D%5Blink%5D%5Cn%5Cn%5Blink%5D%3A+https%3A%2F%2Fexample.com%5Cn%5B%5Efoot%5D%3A+note%22%2C%22to%22%3A%22html5%22%2C%22from%22%3A%22gfm%22%2C%22standalone%22%3Afalse%2C%22embed-resources%22%3Afalse%2C%22table-of-contents%22%3Afalse%2C%22number-sections%22%3Afalse%2C%22citeproc%22%3Afalse%2C%22html-math-method%22%3A%22plain%22%2C%22wrap%22%3A%22auto%22%2C%22highlight-style%22%3Anull%2C%22files%22%3A%7B%7D%2C%22template%22%3Anull%7D) and [pulldown-cmark](https://github.com/pulldown-cmark/pulldown-cmark/blob/fc8fe713f58d7f4495038b48fe76c1f101fb3af1/pulldown-cmark/specs/footnotes.txt#L394) both produce the same result, GitHub actually disagrees and says that `[^foot]` isn't a footnote.

    There's also the way that it bails out when it sees an unescaped `` ` `` or `<`. The real way to handle it requires integration with the rest of the parser. But both of these only kick in when there's *other* warnings about unclosed code spans and invalid HTML.

    </details>

